        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Maoism</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 1935 CE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Thinkers</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a>, its founder; Chen Duxiu, whose New Youth shaped the young Mao; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lin-biao">Lin Biao</a>, whose charismatic reading prevailed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping">Deng Xiaoping</a>, who recast it as “seeking truth from facts”; Charu Majumdar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naxalites">Naxalite movement</a>; Jose Maria Sison, founder of the Communist Party of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abimael-guzman">Abimael Guzmán</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shining-path">Shining Path</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Key texts</span><span class="info-stat-val">Mao’s The Great Union of the Popular Masses (1919), Strategic Problems of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>’s Revolutionary War (December 1936), On Contradiction (revised in 1951), On Practice and On the Correct Handling of Contradictions Among the People; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations-from-chairman-mao-tse-tung">Quotations from Chairman Mao Zedong</a>; Benjamin I. Schwartz’s Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">Communism</a> and the Rise of Mao (1951), which coined the English term</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Core ideas</span><span class="info-stat-val">the peasantry as the main revolutionary force; people’s war, “surrounding the cities from the countryside”; the mass line, “from the masses, to the masses”; contradiction as the motor of all development; New Democracy as the road to socialism; class struggle continuing under socialism, hence cultural revolution; the Three Worlds Theory</span></div>
