    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Mao adapted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism-leninism">Marxism–Leninism</a> to agrarian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> by making the peasants, not the urban workers, the revolutionary class, and by waging a people’s war to surround the cities from the countryside.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> Thought became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a>’s unified ideology in 1945 and its guide in power until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping">Deng Xiaoping</a>’s reforms of 1978; the party’s 1981 resolution laid on Mao the chief responsibility for the “grave ‘Left’ error” of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a>.</li><li>Some historians hold that thirty million people died in the famines of 1958 to 1961 that followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-leap-forward">Great Leap Forward</a>, and Jung Chang and Jon Halliday blame Maoist rule for as many as 70 million deaths in peacetime.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The English word Maoism was coined in 1951 by Benjamin I. Schwartz; Mao himself avoided it and preferred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> Thought, since he saw his ideas as still developing rather than a complete system.</p></aside>
    </section>

