    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1919</span><span class="rf-text">The May Fourth Movement begins with 3,000 students in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> protesting the Versailles Treaty’s concessions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1936</span><span class="rf-text">Mao’s pamphlet Strategic Problems of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>’s Revolutionary War marks his turn away from the Soviet model</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1945</span><span class="rf-text">The Communist Party’s 7th national congress makes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> Thought its unified ideology and writes it into the party constitution</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1958</span><span class="rf-text">The famines that follow the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-leap-forward">Great Leap Forward</a> begin; by some historians’ count thirty million die by 1961</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1967</span><span class="rf-text">The Naxalbari uprising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> begins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naxalites">Naxalite movement</a>, which spreads <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations-from-chairman-mao-tse-tung">Quotations from Chairman Mao Zedong</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1974</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> announces its Three Worlds Theory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1978</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping">Deng Xiaoping</a> begins market reforms and calls the true spirit of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> Thought “seeking truth from facts”</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1981</span><span class="rf-text">The party’s resolution on its history holds Mao chiefly responsible for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a> while upholding his Thought</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/long-march"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/long-march.jpg" alt="Long March" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/long-march">Long March</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Long_March_1934-1935-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Guimard; derivative work by Rowanwindwhistler, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Maoism grew out of a Chinese intellectual tradition that by the early 20th century was defined by iconoclasm and nationalism. A small but significant part of the old elite of landlords and bureaucrats lost faith in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a>, and the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> in 1911 marked the final failure of the Confucian moral order. During the New Culture Movement of 1915–1919 Chen Duxiu’s periodical New Youth called for the “total destruction of the traditions and values of the past”; it profoundly influenced the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a>, whose first published work appeared in its pages.</p><p>Radical anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">imperialism</a> turned into nationalist fervour when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> of 1919 transferred former German lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. The May Fourth Movement began with 3,000 students protesting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution">Russian Revolution</a> drew the intelligentsia toward Marxism: to become a Marxist was one way to reject both the traditions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>’s past and Western domination of its present.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-march">Long March</a> the party was based in the Yan’an Soviet, where from November 1935 Mao produced most of the works later canonised as the Thought of Mao Zedong and won ideological independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. His pamphlet Strategic Problems of China’s Revolutionary War (December 1936) held that China’s circumstances demanded a unique application of Marxist theory, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinicization">Sinicization</a> of Marxism. In 1945 the party’s 7th congress made Mao Zedong Thought its unified ideology.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="ideas">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Core Ideas</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare.jpg" alt="Guerrilla warfare" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">Guerrilla warfare</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:As_Guerrilhas_na_Guerra_Peninsular_(Roque_Gameiro,_Quadros_da_Hist%C3%B3ria_de_Portugal,_1917).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Roque Gameiro, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Maoism departs from European Marxism by putting the countryside before the city. For <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/proletariat">proletariat</a> was the urban working class; for Mao the revolutionary class was the millions of peasants, poor and, in his words, “a clean sheet of paper” on which the newest and most beautiful words could be written. Holding that “political power grows out of the barrel of a gun”, Mao called for a people’s war, in which rural masses waging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> warfare would surround the cities from the countryside. Under New Democracy, a national, popular, anti-feudal and anti-imperialist revolution run by the communists would open the long road to socialism.</p><p>The mass line, “from the masses, to the masses”, asked the party to gather the ideas of the masses, concentrate them in the light of Marxism and return them as a political line, again and again. In On Contradiction and On Practice Mao held that contradiction is present in all things and drives their development, and that each process has a principal contradiction to be tackled first. His view of continuous revolution, first fully theorised in 1956, held that class struggle continues and even intensifies under socialism, so bourgeois ideology and traditional values must be fought constantly.</p><p>The Three Worlds Theory, announced at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> in 1974, cast the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> as an imperialist first world exploiting the third. Many of Maoism’s pillars, such as the distrust of intellectuals, are typical populist ideas.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread &amp; Variants</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sino-soviet-split"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sino-soviet-split.jpg" alt="Sino-Soviet split" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sino-soviet-split">Sino-Soviet split</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mao_Ts%C3%A9-toung,_portrait_en_buste,_assis,_faisant_face_%C3%A0_Nikita_Khrouchtchev,_pendant_la_visite_du_chef_russe_1958_%C3%A0_P%C3%A9kin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown photographerUnknown photographer at the source. Photo distributed by United Press International from files., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sino-soviet-split">Sino-Soviet split</a> of the 1960s each party claimed to be Stalin’s sole heir, and from 1962 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>’s challenge to Soviet leadership divided communist parties around the world. The Albanian Party of Labour and the Communist Parties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, and the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> was the only classic Western communist party to do so. New parties, often named Communist Party (Marxist–Leninist) or Revolutionary Communist Party, grew from the student radicalism of the 1960s and 1970s.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> Mao’s ideas found followers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a> uprising of 1946–1951, and after the Naxalbari uprising of 1967 Charu Majumdar had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations-from-chairman-mao-tse-tung">Quotations from Chairman Mao Zedong</a> read aloud to illiterate peasants. The Communist Party of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> was founded on Mao’s birthday in 1968, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shining-path">Shining Path</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abimael-guzman">Abimael Guzmán</a> waged a prolonged people’s war. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> students of Louis Althusser formed a Maoist circle in 1964, and the Proletarian Left drew in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-paul-sartre">Jean-Paul Sartre</a> and Michel Foucault; in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> Maoists became the largest and most dynamic branch of socialism after 1968, and the Black Panther Party quoted Mao liberally. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-nyerere">Julius Nyerere</a>’s ujamaa in Tanzania drew on Maoist themes of self-reliance and the peasantry.</p><p>After Mao’s death in 1976 the movement split into three camps; today’s international Maoist movement descends from those who denounced Deng’s leadership as traitors.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="power">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">In Power</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/great-leap-forward"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-leap-forward.jpg" alt="Great Leap Forward" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/great-leap-forward">Great Leap Forward</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:People%27s_commone_canteen3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>From the 1950s until the late 1970s Maoism was the political and military ideology of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a>, and its mass campaigns, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anti-rightist-campaign">Anti-Rightist Campaign</a> of 1957–1958, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-leap-forward">Great Leap Forward</a> and the Great Proletarian Cultural Revolution, put Mao’s theory of cultural revolution into practice. Some historians argue that the famines caused by Mao’s land reforms in the Great Leap Forward killed thirty million people between 1958 and 1961; by the end of 1961 malnutrition had nearly halved the birth rate. Party purges and “reeducation” led to the imprisonment or execution of those deemed contrary to Maoist ideals, and Jung Chang and Jon Halliday hold Maoist rule responsible for as many as 70 million deaths in peacetime.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a> Mao claimed that revisionists had infiltrated the government to restore traditionalism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a>, and urged his followers to denounce and expel them, assuring them that the party would not interfere. For roughly ten years those seen as revisionists, chiefly landlords, rich peasants and so-called bourgeois academics, were publicly denounced, and many were beaten, tortured or killed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lin-biao">Lin Biao</a>’s cult of Mao’s charisma, “the atom bomb of the spirit”, became the ruling interpretation; from 1967 Mao and the army began to restrain the mass organisations.</p><p>Abroad, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comoro-islands">Comoros</a> under Ali Soilih was a Maoist state. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> the conflict with Maoist insurgents had cost 13,834 lives by 2018, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shining-path">Shining Path</a>’s insurgency in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> caused tens of thousands of deaths.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="critiques">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Critiques &amp; Debates</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping.jpg" alt="Deng Xiaoping" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping">Deng Xiaoping</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deng_Xiaoping_at_the_arrival_ceremony_for_the_Vice_Premier_of_China_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping">Deng Xiaoping</a> held that Maoism showed the dangers of “ultra-leftism” and the harm done by the mass movements of its era, but he separated the revolutionary Mao from the ruler, judging him “70% right, 30% wrong”. The party’s 1981 resolution laid the chief responsibility for the “grave ‘Left’ error” of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a> on Mao, who had “confused right and wrong and the people with the enemy”. Chinese scholars generally agree that this reading preserves the legitimacy of Communist rule while criticising Mao’s governance; some scholars outside China see it as a justification for restoring the essentials of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a>.</p><p>From within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism-leninism">Marxism–Leninism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enver-hoxha">Enver Hoxha</a> of Albania denounced Deng as a revisionist in 1978 and attacked Maoism itself, arguing that New Democracy halts class struggle, that the Three Worlds Theory is “counter-revolutionary”, and questioning Mao’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> methods. Graham Young notes that Maoist judgements of Stalin range from extremely positive to ambivalent, Martin Cohen sees in Maoism an attempt to combine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a> and socialism, and Slavoj Žižek places Mao’s main contribution in his materialist dialectics. Some Western scholars saw Maoism as a battle to subdue nature and a catastrophe for the environment.</p><p>The death tolls are debated too: Christian Sorace and his co-authors observe that discussions of deaths under Maoism assign responsibility in a way not usual for other ideologies, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberalism">liberalism</a>. Maoists, for their part, contend that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a> strongly deviated from Marxist doctrine.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After Mao’s death in 1976, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping">Deng Xiaoping</a> began market reforms in 1978 and reduced the role of ideology in policy, separating Mao from Maoism and making clear that Mao was fallible. Matthew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galway">Galway</a> writes that the party did not de-Maoize but reassessed Mao’s legacy, stressing his positive contributions while acknowledging his mistakes. The 1981 resolution credited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> Thought with national independence, the transformation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>’s social classes, economic self-sufficiency, the expansion of education and health care and China’s leading role in the Third World, and it remains one of the Four Cardinal Principles of the People’s Republic. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xi-jinping">Xi Jinping</a>, in a speech of 2013, summed it up as seeking truth from facts, the mass line, and national independence and autonomy.</p><p>Contemporary Maoists in China criticise the social inequalities created under the party, and some radicals have backed protests and strikes such as the Jasic incident; Bo Xilai’s mass mobilisation with red songs in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chongqing">Chongqing</a> was called neo-Maoist. In the 2020s the growing wealth gap and the 996 working hours have revived Mao’s thought among China’s generation Z, and the government has censored some Maoist posts.</p><p>Abroad, the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> (Maoist), designated a terrorist organisation in India, the New People’s Army in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> and the TKP/ML in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> still wage armed struggles, while the Workers’ Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> broke formally with its Maoist past in 2008.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Maoism, officially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> Thought, is Mao Zedong’s adaptation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism-leninism">Marxism–Leninism</a> to agrarian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, built on peasant revolution, people’s war and the mass line. It guided the People’s Republic from the 1950s to the late 1970s, through the Great Leap Forward, whose famines killed thirty million by some historians’ count, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a>, and it still inspires insurgencies abroad.</em></p>

